{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789965629,"nonce":"0kgeY8WJhlvS4RMTLZIZduD93Qk=","title":"Python: += and *= on a dynamically typed list build a new list","message":"For a target the frontend types as a list,  and  extend or repeat the list in place and store the same pointer, so aliases see the change. For a target typed Object that holds a list at run time, the lowering still goes through zb_any_arith, which builds a new list: an alias bound before the statement keeps the old one.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    def f(xs):\n        alias = xs\n        xs += [1]\n        return alias is xs\n\n    print(f(list(range(3))))   # CPython True\n\nThe fix is a dynamic in-place arithmetic entry in zyntax_builtins (dynamic.rs) that extends or repeats a boxed list of any element kind in place and falls back to zb_any_arith otherwise, and a call to it from combine_in_place in crates/zyntax_python/src/lower.rs when the held value is Object.","files":null}]}